// BUG REPRO (entity-type swap): the temporal type-checker enforces operand
// types on `==`/`!=`, but does NOT type-check a predicate FIELD PATTERN whose
// declared type is an entity type. Here `Tool2::request{ input.mode: ... }`
// declares `input.mode : Drupe::Tool2_Input_mode`, but the pattern binds
// it to an entity literal of a DIFFERENT type, `Tool1_Input_mode::"read"`.
//
// `"read"` IS a permitted id of `Tool1_Input_mode`, so the enum-eid check in
// `check_entity_types` passes it — the ONLY thing that should reject this is
// the field-argument type check (`check_arg_type`), which must see the
// expected type `entity:Tool2_Input_mode` and the actual `entity:Tool1_Input_mode`
// and reject the mismatch. It currently does not fire for `input.<field>`
// patterns (the arg name is looked up as the dotted string "input.mode" inside
// the `input` record, which never matches), so this policy validates cleanly
// when it should be rejected. This can cause a silent runtime failure: the
// wrong-typed literal never matches a real Tool2 event.
//
// NOTE — this is a TEMPORAL-DIALECT-SPECIFIC rejection, stricter than Cedar.
// The temporal checker's `types_compatible`/`param_accepts` require EQUAL
// entity tags, so a mismatched-entity `==` or field pattern is a hard type
// error (verified: the temporal `==` path already errors on `OAuthUser` vs
// `Tool1_Input_mode`). Cedar's own validator, by contrast, treats any
// `entity == entity` as well-typed and downgrades this to an "impossible
// policy" WARNING (see 1039/1040 for the scalar variants Cedar *does* reject
// as errors; the entity-swap Cedar analogue is intentionally NOT an
// expected-failure). Do not "align" this to Cedar's warning behavior.
//
// Expected: rejected at `validate` by the temporal type-checker.
permit (principal, action == Drupe::Action::"Tool1", resource)
when temporal {
    formerly within 1h Drupe::Action::"Tool2"::request{
        input.mode: Drupe::Tool1_Input_mode::"read"
    }
};
